    "body": "Owner instruction: make sure all of cloudstore is pushed to icloud. Index 397 to 572 objects, nothing deleted or overwritten, 1.8 GB of verified staging reclaimed. TWO FINDINGS: the chat corpus chat-v0-p2 - which every experiment in this session ran on - had NO archive at all while the store looked complete by object count, as did inputs/ and step0/; and workspace's 42 existing objects resolve to icloud:X10, the SSD with the defective flash region, so the largest archive was on the failing device. Its contents now exist on iCloud proper as 12 ws-* objects. THREE AUDIT BLIND SPOTS, each invisible to the previous check: name matching misses containers; timestamps catch stale archives (and because cloud-store refuses to overwrite, catching up needs a DATED NAME - which is why workspace-2026-10-01 exists); lab labels hide directories archived as many labelled objects. Both error directions occur. THE DISK ARITHMETIC: cloud-store stages a tar equal to the directory size, so a push needs about twice its size free; pushing workspace at once drove free disk from 18 GiB to 5.0 GiB and the job was killed, restoring 18 GiB - correct, because the disk floor is an owner cap outside the never-cancel protection. THE FIX IS STRUCTURAL: recurse to sub-directory granularity and check 2*size + 10 GiB <= free before each object, after which workspace pushed as 12 objects with one logged skip and uor-r4-lab as 41 with zero skips and zero failures. RESIDUE: one 16 KB staging shell capturing a snapshot-preserved directory owned with restrictive permissions; no unique data, left alone rather than forcing deletion of another lab preserved material. References #820",
